Third independent pass (fresh adversarial audit of the shipped implementation, per protocol "second independent pass" escalation):
- F1 (MAJOR): `_resolve_vintages` DROPPED rejected vintage rows whenever at least one other version of the same observation was allowed — the revision's rejection vanished from the audit trail, breaking auditability (a reviewer could not see the rejected release). Fixed: `rest` now keeps rows with null vintage_date OR disallowed (rejected rows survive as `excluded`), and the allowed/not-allowed resolution no longer destroys them. Regression tests: rejected revision preserved at evaluate level and snapshot level; adversarial end-to-end revision test; monotonic-information invariant.
- F2 (MAJOR): market payloads contained RETROACTIVELY split-adjusted OHLCV presented as as-published values (verified on real DS-000001 data: 2014-06-06 AAPL close 23.05607 is the post-7:1/4:1 value; the real published close was 645.57 = x28). A pre-split historical snapshot was silently carrying post-split prices — a quantitative backtest leak. Fixed: `market_timing_frame` reconstructs as-published OHLCV from the sibling `events.parquet` (raw = adjusted x product of split ratios with ex_date > trade_date; raw volume = volume / factor; ex-date itself is NOT undone); payload gains `price_basis` = `as_published` or explicit `provider_split_adjusted` when no events artifact exists; `adjclose`/`adjustment` excluded; the engine auto-loads the sibling events artifact. Regression tests: unit reconstruction (x28, x4, factor-1), provider-basis fallback, engine snapshot with real split events, adversarial future-split leak test.
- F3 (MAJOR): `effective_time` was parsed but never enforced — a record public at t but only applicable after t would be admitted. Fixed: new `EFFECTIVE_AFTER_AS_OF` gate in both `decide()` and `evaluate()` (rule 4), documented as a forward contract (current sources: effective == publication). Regression tests: after/at/before gates incl. a second-gate scenario.
- F4 (MINOR): `evaluate()` vs `decide()` inconsistency — unknown `series_policy` values and precision case variants ("DATE" vs "date") were handled differently between the vectorized engine and the rule loop. Fixed: engine policy allow-list now matches the rule loop (`not in ["", "non_revised"]`), precision is casefolded, invalid precision values raise ValueError instead of silently defaulting. Regression tests: policy-agreement + precision-case + invalid-precision.
- F5 (MINOR): contract config conventions (`exchange_tz`, `session_close`, ...) were parsed and ignored. Fixed: `IMPLEMENTED_CONVENTIONS` validation raises ValueError on any convention the engine does not enforce. Regression test: mismatched session_close rejected at load.
- F6: release calendar (prompt §8) was absent. Implemented: `ReleaseCalendar`/`ReleaseCalendarEntry` with `enabled`/`evidence`/weekday guards; FRED timing sharpens a release to the scheduled instant (08:30 America/New_York) only for enabled+weekday-matching entries, else the next-day convention; `"release_calendar": []` in default config. Regression tests: absent/disabled/enabled EST/enabled EDT/weekday-mismatch.
- All fixes verified: 207 tests pass (46 rules, 42 engine, 20 leaks + Phase 1-3), no Phase 1-3 regression.
Final verdict: PASS — implementation, tests, documentation, and architecture consistent with the ORBIT roadmap.



